

### 1. What is state, and what is its setter? (reminder)

```jsx
const [item, setItem] = useState({ quantity: 1, giftWrap: false });
```

Until now, state held simple things: a number like `0`, or a name like `"John"`. But state can hold anything, including an object.

What is an object? It is a box that groups several values together. Think of a coffee mug in a shopping cart. It has a quantity (1 mug) and a gift wrap (yes or no). Instead of two separate variables, you put both inside one box: `{ quantity: 1, giftWrap: false }`.

The rules stay the same. `item` is the variable. `setItem` is the setter. When you want a change, you call `setItem`.

Something that can be tricky: the change does not happen on that line. React first calls your component again, and only then does `item` hold the new value.

### 2. What happens when you give the setter the same value? (reminder)

```jsx
setScore(2); // score is already 2
```

Every time you call a setter, React asks itself one question: "Is this the same value I already have?"

Say the score is 2 and you call `setScore(2)`. It is the same value, so React does nothing. There is no new render, and the screen stays the same. Why? Because the screen already shows 2. There is no work to do.

React checks this with a JavaScript tool called `Object.is`. Remember this question, "is it the same value?", because in this lesson it decides everything.

### 3. What is a mutation?

```jsx
item.quantity = 2;
```

A number cannot change from the inside: 0 is always 0. When you call `setScore(5)`, you do not turn 0 into 5. You replace it with 5.

An object is different. It is a box, and you can open the box and change what is inside: `item.quantity = 2`. This is called a mutation. The box stays the same box, and only its contents change. `item.quantity++` and `item.quantity += 1` are mutations too.

Something that can be tricky: JavaScript allows this, and React does not stop you. There is no error and no warning. But you never called the setter, so React has no idea anything changed. The screen stays the same.

So in React, treat every object in state as read-only. You read it. You never write into it. You replace it.

### 4. What is the difference between the same object and a new object?

```jsx
setItem(item);                             // the same box
setItem({ quantity: 2, giftWrap: false }); // a new box
```

Remember React's question: "Is this the same value?" For an object, React never opens the box to look at the fields. It only asks: "Is this the very same box?"

So if you mutate `item` and then call `setItem(item)`, React sees the same box and does nothing. The quantity inside really is 2, but the card still says 1.

The curly braces `{ ... }` build a brand-new box. React sees a different box and renders. Now the card says 2.

And here is the strange part: the mutation does not disappear. It waits. The next time another click renders the card, such as `Add gift wrap`, the hidden quantity suddenly jumps onto the screen.

So to summarize: a changed box and a new box can hold the same numbers, but only the new box reaches the screen.

### 5. What is object spread?

```jsx
setItem({ ...item, giftWrap: true });
```

Writing every field by hand gets long. A mug with two fields is fine. A product with ten fields is not.

The three dots `...item` copy every field of `item` into the new box. You already saw them on JSX tags, like `<ArticleCard {...article} />` (Lesson 9). They do the same job here.

Why copy the old fields at all? Because a setter replaces the whole object. It does not merge. `setItem({ giftWrap: true })` throws away the whole old box and keeps only the gift wrap. The quantity is gone.

Something that can be tricky: order matters. JavaScript fills the box from left to right, and the last value wins. `{ ...item, giftWrap: true }` turns the gift wrap on. `{ giftWrap: true, ...item }` copies the old `false` back over it. The rule is: spread first, change last.

### 6. What is local mutation? (reminder)

```jsx
const nextItem = { ...item };
nextItem.quantity = 2;
setItem(nextItem);
```

So is every `=` on an object forbidden? No.

If you just built a new box inside the same function, you can change it freely, because nobody else is looking at it yet. It is like packing a parcel before you hand it to the post office. Nobody has seen it, so nobody gets confused.

This code does exactly the same thing as `setItem({ ...item, quantity: 2 })`.

The catch is that the box must really be new. And what about everything inside it? Keep that question for the next definition.

### 7. What is a shallow copy?

```jsx
const item = {
  quantity: 1,
  delivery: { address: '12 Main Street', time: 'Morning' }
};
```

Sometimes a box holds another box. Here, `item` holds a `delivery` box.

Something that can be tricky: the outer box does not really contain the inner box. It only holds a note with the inner box's address. So there are two different boxes, and one points at the other.

When you spread, `{ ...item }`, you get a new outer box, but only a copy of the note. Both outer boxes now point at the same `delivery` box. That is why it is called a shallow copy: it copies only the top level.

Reading is safe. Writing is not. `nextItem.delivery.time = 'Evening'` writes into the shared box, so the starting object changes too. After that, `Start over` cannot bring back Morning.

The fix is to copy every box on the way down:

```jsx
setItem({ ...item, delivery: { ...item.delivery, time: 'Evening' } });
```

### 8. What is the event object? (reminder)

```jsx
function handleChange(e) {
  console.log(e.target.name, e.target.value);
}

<input name="message" onChange={handleChange} />
```

When the user clicks or types, React calls your handler and hands it the event object, usually named `e`. You never pass it yourself. React does.

`e.target` is the element where it happened. Until now, that was a button. Now it is a text box.

Every time the user types a letter, React calls `handleChange`. `e.target.value` is the text in the box right now, like `Hi`. `e.target.name` is the `name` you wrote on the tag, like `message`.

The catch: `e.target.name` is just a string you wrote on the tag. It is not a field of `item`. It matches a field only if you make it match.

### 9. What is a computed property name?

```jsx
setItem({ ...item, [e.target.name]: e.target.value });
```

Usually you write the field name directly: `{ message: 'Hi' }`. But what if you only know the name when the code runs?

Square brackets solve this. Write `const field = 'message'`, and then `{ [field]: 'Hi' }` means: "read the variable first, and use its value as the name." The result is `{ message: 'Hi' }`.

Without the brackets, `{ field: 'Hi' }`, JavaScript reads no variable. It uses the word "field" itself, so you get a field literally named `field`.

What is cool: one handler can now serve every text box. Type in the box named `message`, and `item.message` changes. Type in the box named `recipient`, and `item.recipient` changes.

The catch: the `name` on the tag must match the field letter for letter. If you write `name="msg"`, the handler creates a new field that nothing reads. The `Message:` line on the card stays empty, and there is no error.

